{"title":"Alder Grand Hotel","sub":"How many extra bookings should a full hotel take?","type":"Pricing","industry":"Hospitality","geo":"United States","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Overbooking · expected value · probability","prompt":"The Alder Grand is a 300-room conference hotel in Washington, DC. On busy weeknights it sells out, but about 8% of guests with bookings don't show up, leaving rooms empty that could have been sold. The revenue manager wants to accept extra bookings beyond 300. The general manager worries about turning away guests who do arrive. How many extra bookings should the hotel take?","clarify":[{"q":"Room economics?","a":"Rate about $250 a night; about $45 of costs per occupied room."},{"q":"No-shows?","a":"On busy weeknights, about 8% of booked guests don't arrive, independently of each other."},{"q":"Walking a guest?","a":"If more guests arrive than there are rooms, the hotel pays for a room at a nearby hotel plus transport and a goodwill voucher: about $400 per guest, plus reputational damage."},{"q":"Cancellation policy?","a":"No-shows aren't charged on most corporate rates."}],"structure":{"root":"How many bookings beyond 300 should the Alder Grand accept?","branches":[{"head":"Benefit of an extra booking","items":["Fills a no-show room: $205 margin"]},{"head":"Cost of an extra booking","items":["Walking a guest: $400"]},{"head":"Probability","items":["How many no-shows on a given night"]},{"head":"Other factors","items":["Reputation","Who gets walked"]}]},"hypothesis":"With 8% no-shows, about 24 rooms go empty on a sold-out night, but the number varies. Since walking a guest costs more than a room earns, the hotel should stop a little short of the bookings that would, on average, exactly fill the hotel.","questions":[{"q":"A simple answer would be to overbook by the expected number of no-shows. What is that, and what's wrong with it?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>To expect exactly 300 arrivals, book 300 ÷ 92% ≈ <b>326</b>, i.e. 26 extra. But arrivals vary from night to night, so on about half of those nights more than 300 guests would turn up.</p> <p>An extra booking that fills an empty room earns $205; one that arrives to a full hotel costs $400. Because the downside is bigger than the upside, the hotel should stop a little short of 26.</p>"},{"q":"Here is expected profit per night for different numbers of extra bookings. What do you recommend?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"393.86pt\" height=\"171.7pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 393.86 171.7\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 171.7 L 393.86 171.7 L 393.86 0 L 0 0 L 0 171.7 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 40.7 145.8 L 386.66 145.8 L 386.66 7.2 L 40.7 7.2 L 40.7 145.8 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"ma99844d2ee\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #4a5568; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#ma99844d2ee\" x=\"56.425455\" y=\"145.8\" style=\"fill: #4a5568; stroke: #4a5568; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #4a5568\" transform=\"translate(53.725455 162.025) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-38\" d=\"M 1920 -77 Q 1389 -77 1046 198 Q 704 474 544 989 Q 384 1504 384 2234 Q 384 2963 544 3481 Q 704 4000 1046 4272 Q 1389 4544 1920 4544 Q 2458 4544 2797 4272 Q 3136 4000 3296 3481 Q 3456 2963 3456 2234 Q 3456 1504 3296 989 Q 3136 474 2797 198 Q 2458 -77 1920 -77 z\nM 1920 390 Q 2272 390 2483 579 Q 2694 768 2793 1104 Q 2893 1440 2893 1869 L 2893 2598 Q 2893 3034 2793 3366 Q 2694 3699 2483 3888 Q 2272 4077 1920 4077 Q 1574 4077 1360 3888 Q 1146 3699 1046 3366 Q 947 3034 947 2598 L 947 1869 Q 947 1440 1046 1104 Q 1146 768 1360 579 Q